// @test: run-interpreter
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: types, layout, field-access, startup
// @expected-stdout: 10 ok
// @skip: T0815 is open — reading a member named `size` off a local variable
//        aborts in global_ctors before main. This spec states the
//        expectation and is the acceptance control for that task; the skip
//        comes off in the commit that fixes the read, not by renaming the
//        member.
//
// Pin (T0815) — `size` is an ordinary member name.
//
// Today this file dies before main, with no source location and no mention
// of `size`:
//
//     error: VBC global_ctors error: LayoutMismatch { type_id: TypeId(515),
//     tag: 0, got_field_count: 0, expected_field_count: None,
//     reason: "unknown type_id" }
//
// `verum check` reports nothing either way, so this can only be pinned by
// RUNNING.
//
// `size` is alone in this. Tested as both field and method against the same
// template: alignment, stride and bits are fine — though they sit in the
// same reserved list at crates/verum_types/src/infer/expr.rs:9042 — and so
// are len, new, clone, hash, next, value, data, name, kind, index, count
// and capacity. The lead is that a member read named `size` is routed to
// the layout-property path at
// crates/verum_vbc/src/codegen/expressions.rs:20628 — the one answering
// `SomeType.size` as a sizeof query — instead of to a field read.
//
// THE CONTROL IS DELIBERATELY IN A SEPARATE FILE.
// `member_named_size_via_call.vr` reads the same member off a CALL and
// passes today. Putting the two in one file makes BOTH work: a single
// `build().size` anywhere in the program is enough to make the local read
// succeed as well, which is itself the sharpest clue about the mechanism —
// and which silently destroys any spec that tries to carry its own control
// here.

type Sized is { size: Int };

fn main() {
    let s = Sized { size: 10 };
    print(f"{s.size} ok");
}
